From: DKR-R Windows 95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Enqueue a screen update only when a frame has been drawn

The vertical-interval thread posts a ScreenUpdateAction every retrace and the
queue keeps at most one pending, which was assumed to be enough coalescing.
Measured on 22 September 2026 it is not: 3,371 of them arrive for 389 rendered
frames -- 8.7 per frame -- each costing about a millisecond of the graphics
thread, for a handler that assigns two registers, clears a flag and calls a
function that increments a counter.

Dropping them is safe on this target because nothing reads what they carry:
`ultramodern::renderer::get_vi_regs` has no caller in this tree, RT64 being the
consumer and RT64 not being built for Windows 95, and `update_screen` neither
presents nor draws. The first sixteen go through unconditionally all the same,
because the very first is what starts the recompiled game and nothing has been
drawn yet to arm the flag.

Measured with the control on the same binary, truncated to the same number of
frame reports:

  every retrace     period 190.2 ms   render 38.2 ms   elsewhere 152.0 ms
  only on a frame   period 184.9 ms   render 38.2 ms   elsewhere 146.8 ms

-2.8% on the frame. The renderer's own figure is identical, so the whole of it
is in `elsewhere`: 5.2 ms a frame. 386 updates enqueued against 3,180 skipped.

On by default where it was measured, off elsewhere, and `DKR_VI_PRESENT=every`
or `=new` overrides either way. The choice is announced once in the log, because
a switch that silently fails to take and a switch that changes nothing are the
same measurement.

---
--- a/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
@@ -138,6 +138,53 @@
     moodycamel::ConcurrentQueue<OSThread*> deleted_threads{};
 } events_context{};
 
+/* **Enqueue a screen update only when there is a new frame to show.**
+ *
+ * The vertical-interval thread posts one of these every retrace and the queue
+ * keeps at most one pending, which was thought to be enough. Measured on
+ * 22 September 2026 it is not: 3,371 of them arrive for 389 rendered frames --
+ * **8.7 per frame** -- each costing about a millisecond of the graphics thread
+ * for a handler that assigns two registers, clears a flag and increments a
+ * counter. Four percent of the wall, buying nothing that one update would not.
+ *
+ * What makes it safe to drop them here is that nothing on this target reads what
+ * they carry: `ultramodern::renderer::get_vi_regs` has no caller in this tree --
+ * RT64 is the consumer and RT64 is not built for Windows 95 -- and
+ * `update_screen` neither presents nor draws. The first updates are let through
+ * unconditionally all the same, because the very first is what starts the
+ * recompiled game.
+ *
+ * Behind a switch and announced once, because a switch that silently fails to
+ * take and a switch that changes nothing are the same measurement. */
+static std::atomic<bool> g_frame_drawn_since_update{true};
+static std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_updates_enqueued{0};
+static std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_updates_skipped{0};
+
+static bool update_only_on_new_frame(void) {
+    static int decided = -1;
+    if (decided < 0) {
+        /* On by default where it was measured and where nothing reads what the
+           action carries; off everywhere else, because a target that builds RT64
+           has a consumer for those registers and this has not been measured
+           against one. `DKR_VI_PRESENT=every` restores the old behaviour, which
+           is how the two were compared. */
+#ifdef DKR_TARGET_WIN95
+        decided = 1;
+#else
+        decided = 0;
+#endif
+        const char* choice = std::getenv("DKR_VI_PRESENT");
+        if (choice != nullptr) {
+            decided = (choice[0] == 'n') ? 1 : ((choice[0] == 'e') ? 0 : decided);
+        }
+        std::fprintf(stderr, "[vi][present] %s (DKR_VI_PRESENT=%s)\n",
+                     decided ? "only when a frame has been drawn"
+                             : "every retrace",
+                     (choice != nullptr) ? choice : "unset");
+    }
+    return decided == 1;
+}
+
 ultramodern::renderer::ViRegs* ultramodern::renderer::get_vi_regs() {
     return &events_context.vi.update_screen_regs;
 }
@@ -230,7 +277,18 @@
         // presents. Keep at most one pending update so graphics tasks cannot
         // age behind stale screen-update work.
         if (!screen_update_action_pending.exchange(true)) {
-            events_context.action_queue.enqueue(ScreenUpdateAction{ events_context.vi.regs });
+            /* The first sixteen go through whatever the policy: the first one
+               starts the recompiled game, and nothing has been drawn yet to
+               arm the flag. */
+            const unsigned long long sent = g_updates_enqueued.load();
+            const bool obliged = !update_only_on_new_frame() || sent < 16ULL;
+            if (obliged || g_frame_drawn_since_update.exchange(false)) {
+                g_updates_enqueued.fetch_add(1);
+                events_context.action_queue.enqueue(ScreenUpdateAction{ events_context.vi.regs });
+            } else {
+                g_updates_skipped.fetch_add(1);
+                screen_update_action_pending.store(false);
+            }
         }
 
         // Update VI registers and swap VI modes.
